name: reading files
boss: CatMan
flavor: Everything you wrote, I read back.
brief: cat dumps a whole file. head and tail take the ends of it. tac reverses
brief: it. less lets you move around inside one too big to dump. strings pulls
brief: the readable text out of a file that is not text at all.
brief:
brief: The habit worth building: reach for head before cat. A file you did not
brief: write can be a gigabyte, and cat will happily send all of it.
---
q: {print|show|dump} the contents of @txt@
a: cat @txt@
out: rotate the logs
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: no flags -- cat just dumps the whole file to the screen
---
q: %show% @txt@ with cat and line numbers, to quote one back
a: cat -n @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: cat
teach: cat -n
hint: n numbers every line, including blanks
---
q: %show% @cfg@ with cat, to spot trailing whitespace on each line
a: cat -e @cfg@
cmp: ref
ref: cat
teach: cat -e
hint: cat calls this "End each line with $"
---
q: %show% the first ten lines of @log@
a: head @log@
out: service started
ref: head
teach: head
hint: ten is what head does with no arguments
---
q: %show% the first @n@ lines of @log@ with head
a: head -n @n@ @log@
a: head -@n@ @log@
out: service started
ref: head
teach: head -n
hint: -n takes a count, or glue the number straight onto the dash
---
q: %show% @log@'s date header with head -- the first 20 bytes
a: head -c 20 @log@
out: 2024-03-01
ref: head
teach: head -c
hint: c for characters
---
q: %show% the last ten lines of @log@
a: tail @log@
out: out of memory
ref: tail
teach: tail
hint: ten is what tail does with no arguments, same as head
---
q: %show% the last @n3@ lines of @log@ with tail
a: tail -n @n3@ @log@
a: tail -@n3@ @log@
cmp: ref
ref: tail
teach: tail -n
hint: -n takes a count, same syntax as head -n
---
q: %show% @log@ with tail, starting at line 5 -- not the last 5
a: tail -n +5 @log@
out: connection refused
ref: tail
teach: tail -n
hint: a plus sign in front of the number changes it from "last N" to "from N"
---
q: {watch @log@, printing new lines as they are appended|follow @log@, printing each new line as it's appended|keep @log@ open, printing new lines as they arrive}, with tail
a: tail -f @log@
why: interactive
ref: tail
teach: tail -f
hint: f for follow. this one never exits -- Ctrl+c is how you leave.
---
q: flip @txt@ so the last line reads first
a: tac @txt@
out: verify the root hash
ref: tac
teach: tac
hint: it is cat backwards, which is also what it does
---
q: {pull the readable text out of @bin@, which is not a text file|extract the printable text from @bin@, a non-text file|pull the human-readable strings out of the binary @bin@}
a: strings @bin@
out: unlocked-by-strings
ref: strings
teach: strings
hint: pulls sequences of printable characters out of any file, text or not
---
q: %show% strings in @bin@, skipping noise under 5 characters
a: strings -n 5 @bin@
out: unlocked-by-strings
ref: strings
teach: strings -n
hint: -n sets the minimum run length to count as a string
---
q: %show% the strings in @bin@ with each one's byte offset
a: strings -t d @bin@
a: strings -t x @bin@
out: unlocked-by-strings
ref: strings
teach: strings -t
hint: t takes a radix -- d decimal, x hex, o octal
---
q: {open @log@ in the pager|page through @log@|view @log@ a screen at a time}
a: less @log@
why: interactive
ref: less
teach: less
hint: q leaves, /pattern searches, g and G jump to the ends
---
q: {page @log@ in less with line numbers, so you can point one out|open @log@ in less with line numbers, to reference one|view @log@ in less with line numbers, to call one out}
a: less -N @log@
why: interactive
ref: less
teach: less -N
hint: N turns on line numbers in the margin
---
q: {view @log@ in less without wrapping, columns stay aligned|page @log@ in less, unwrapped -- keep columns aligned|open @log@ in less without wrapping long lines}
a: less -S @log@
why: interactive
ref: less
teach: less -S
hint: S stops wrapping -- long lines scroll sideways instead
---
q: {open @log@ in less, searching case-insensitively|page @log@ in less, with case-insensitive search|view @log@ in less, so searches ignore case}
a: less -I @log@
why: interactive
ref: less
teach: less -I
hint: capital I makes searches ignore case
---
q: %show% @log@'s last 20 bytes with tail -- just how it ended
a: tail -c 20 @log@
cmp: ref
ref: tail
teach: tail -c
hint: c for characters/bytes, same idea as head -c
---
q: {follow @log@ by name with tail, so it survives rotation|follow @log@ by name not handle, with tail -- survives rotation|watch @log@ with tail, in a way that survives rotation}
a: tail -F @log@
why: interactive
ref: tail
teach: tail -F
hint: capital F reopens the name; lowercase f holds the old file open forever
